<CaseLayout
  title="Colla parte"
  years="2026"
  summary="A Mac app that turns the choir director’s PDF into checked sheet music and a practice MP3 for my own voice."
  meta={[
    ['Role', 'Problem, requirements, every design decision, testing with real pieces'],
    ['Built with', 'Claude Code as AI pair programmer'],
    ['Period', '10 days, 18–27 Oct 2026'],
    ['Proof', '116 automated tests; on a test piece with known notes, 0 of 124 voice-bars wrong'],
    ['Status', 'In use for my choir. Not on GitHub: the test scores are under copyright'],
  ]}
  film="notenprogramm"
  stage="notenprogramm"
  next={{ href: '/work/stub/', title: 'Stub' }}
>
  <section>
    <h2>The itch</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">I learn my choir part by ear while I work, and I made those practice tracks by hand.</p>
    <p>
      I sing tenor in a choir. The music arrives as a PDF from the choir director, and I learn my part by listening to a
      practice track while I work, until I know the melody by heart.
    </p>
    <p>
      Software that reads sheet music exists, but it makes mistakes, and the tools around it are complicated. To trust
      the result I would have had to check every bar against the original myself. I wanted the opposite: a program that
      tells me which few notes it is unsure about, in my voice, and lets me fix them right there.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Walkthrough</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">From the choir director’s PDF to the practice track.</p>
    <Walkthrough steps={steps} note="Public-domain pieces only · German UI" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What didn’t work</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">Every real piece broke something. Each break changed the program.</p>
    <p>
      I tested with what my choir actually sings, from a Renaissance double choir to Whitacre, and kept a dated note of
      every failure. Where there is a number, it was measured on the piece that failed, before and after the change.
    </p>
    <FixList
      fixes={[
        {
          date: '18 Oct',
          broke: 'The first version wrote a check report: every bar as a picture, errors in colour. I told Claude: that’s no use to me, I want to answer in the program and never fix anything in MuseScore afterwards.',
          changed: 'The report was dropped the same evening. An editor took its place: click a note, change it, Enter confirms the bar.',
        },
        {
          date: '18 Oct',
          broke: 'A Bach chorale for four voices came out with eight. A few misread note values made notes overlap, and the voice count came from the busiest bar.',
          changed: 'Voices are counted by majority across all bars.',
          numbers: ['8 voices', '4'],
        },
        {
          date: '18 Oct',
          broke: 'One reader alone (homr) misread durations, so in a Bach chorale eight bars did not add up. A third engine, oemer, took over four minutes a page and made the same kind of errors.',
          changed: 'Two readers check each other: Audiveris reads the skeleton, homr cross-checks. Where they disagree, I am asked.',
          numbers: ['8 bars off', '0'],
        },
        {
          date: '18 Oct',
          broke: 'Poulenc’s “Salve Regina” came out with 164 bars and five voices instead of 57 and four: a publisher’s catalogue page, parallel runs that blocked each other, missed meter changes.',
          changed: 'Bars are matched by their position on the page, bar lengths are decided by vote, pages are read one by one with a watchdog.',
          numbers: ['164 bars', '57'],
        },
        {
          date: '19 Oct',
          broke: 'My own pass through four Poulenc voices: both real errors had been asked, but 15 ties were missing from the result.',
          changed: 'Slurs between two equal notes become ties.',
          numbers: ['15 ties lost', '0'],
        },
        {
          date: '20 Oct',
          broke: 'A test piece where every note is known (two Bach chorales, engraved by MuseScore) showed that key changes were ignored.',
          changed: 'The key is tracked bar by bar.',
          numbers: ['44 wrong notes', '4'],
        },
        {
          date: '22 Oct',
          broke: 'Victoria’s double-choir “Ave Maria” opens with incipits in old clefs. They became extra bars: 194 instead of 92, and 642 open questions.',
          changed: 'The incipits are blanked out before reading.',
          numbers: ['194 bars', '92'],
        },
        {
          date: '23 Oct',
          broke: 'An idea: blank out the sung text before reading, to help recognition. Measured on five pieces, four got more open questions, Schumann more than three times as many.',
