          date: '26 Oct',
          broke: 'The MP3 export failed on Parry, bar 38: MuseScore stopped with “code 40” on triplets that do not close.',
          changed: 'The MP3 is made from a copy rounded to sixteenths; the MusicXML stays exact.',
        },
        {
          date: '27 Oct',
          broke: 'The start page said 22 open questions, the editor 25.',
          changed: 'Both count the same way, checked on every piece.',
          numbers: ['22 vs 25', 'same'],
        },
        {
          date: 'Open',
          broke: 'Both readers read a breve, a note of two whole bars, as a whole note.',
          changed: 'A key sets a breve by hand. The reading itself is not fixed.',
          open: true,
        },
      ]}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Can I trust it?</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">Check, don’t retype: I answer a handful of questions instead of checking every bar.</p>
    <p>
      The two readers rarely make the same mistake, so the questions sit where errors are likely. On the test piece with
      known notes, nothing is wrong after reading; all eleven questions only ask me to confirm. When I checked 57 bars of
      four Poulenc voices by hand, no error in pitch, duration or accidentals had slipped through unasked.
    </p>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <ScrubFigure kind="noten-questions" ratio="1200 / 580" narrow="600 / 900" />
      <figcaption>
        Schumann’s “An die Sterne”, 83 bars for eight voices, on 26 Oct: most voices had one to three questions, one had
        22 because a whole line had been read an octave off. With one question per line it now has 10.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <dl class="c-numbers">
      <div><dt>0 / 124</dt><dd>voice-bars wrong on the test piece with known notes</dd></div>
      <div><dt>374</dt><dd>bars found on the PDF page, all of them, in the 6 pieces checked</dd></div>
      <div><dt>116</dt><dd>automated tests, all passing</dd></div>
      <div><dt>7 s</dt><dd>for MusicXML and a practice MP3 of a four-minute piece</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Reading is the slow part: one to three minutes per page on a laptop, while I do something else. How many questions
      a voice gets depends on the print: one to three for most of the Schumann voices, a few dozen for Victoria’s
      double choir.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Decisions</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">Right notes over fewer questions, and the program on my Mac only.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Accuracy over fewer questions.</strong> In my Poulenc pass the questions about notes only one reader had
      seen found nothing. Dropping them would have cut the questions to about a third. I kept them: pitch, duration and
      accidentals matter more to me than ties, so ties are not cross-checked, notes are.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>All voices equally loud.</strong> Claude assumed my voice should be louder in the practice track. I said no:
      I want to hear where my line sits in the chord. Every voice was measured and set within one loudness unit; mine
      plays on harmonica, the others on flute, clarinet and horn. That is where the name comes from: colla parte, in
      music, means an instrument plays along with the voice.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Local, and not on GitHub.</strong> The PDFs and the practice tracks stay on the Mac, nothing goes to a
      cloud. I considered publishing the code, but the test scores are under copyright and two of the libraries are
      AGPL, so it stays private.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>From “looks like AI” to a choir folder</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">The first interface looked like every AI-built app, so I had three directions drawn up.</p>
    <p>
      An audit named the patterns: blue pill buttons, identical cards with soft shadows, a dashed “+ New” tile, status
      dots. The three directions were a piano score, a choir folder with pencil, and plain Mac-native. I chose the choir
      folder: a black frame with white sheets inside. Pencil grey means “still to check”, highlighter yellow only “my
      voice”, red pencil only real errors, and there is no blue at all. The type is Edwin, in the style of Century
      Schoolbook, with music symbols from Bravura.
    </p>
    <div class="c-pair" style={row(beforeEditor, afterEditor)}>
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={beforeEditor}
          alt="The editor on 23 October: Bach’s “O Food to Pilgrims Given”, tenor, bar 25, with blue buttons and a sidebar full of tools."
          widths={full([640, 1280], beforeEditor)}
          sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 45vw"
        />
        <figcaption>Before, 23 Oct: every tool visible at once.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={afterEditor}
          alt="The same bar and question in today’s editor: the original and the transcription side by side like an open folder, the question underneath."
          widths={full([640, 1280, 2560], afterEditor)}
          sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 45vw"
        />
        <figcaption>After: the same question, bar and window size today.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="c-trio">
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={beforeUi}
          alt="The first version: score thumbnails as cards with status dots, a blue rounded button “PDF öffnen” and a pill-shaped play button."
          widths={full([430], beforeUi)}
          sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 30vw"
        />
        <figcaption>Before: blue pills, cards and status dots.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={pianoScore}
          alt="Direction one: a white page titled “Chorpartituren” with dark blue covers for each piece, set like a printed score."
          widths={full([440, 880], pianoScore)}
          sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 30vw"
        />
        <figcaption>Not taken: a piano score.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={macNative}
          alt="Direction three: a Mac-native window with a sidebar of filters and the scores as a grid of thumbnails."
          widths={full([460, 920], macNative)}
          sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 30vw"
        />
        <figcaption>Not taken: Mac-native.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <p>
      For an uncertain note I turned down a pencil circle and tested three marks: grey like a pencil sketch, brackets
      like urtext editions, and a question mark. Brackets won. The correction screen had three layouts too; the open
      folder won, and the tool opens right at the note instead of as a wall of buttons.
    </p>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <Image
        src={marks}
        alt="Three ways to mark an uncertain note on a highlighted line: drawn in grey pencil, in square brackets, or with a small question mark above it."
        widths={full([800, 1600], marks)}
        sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 75vw"
      />
      <figcaption>Three marks for an uncertain note. Brackets won, the way urtext editions mark doubtful notes.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Built with Claude Code</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">My part was the problem, the decisions and the testing; Claude Code wrote the code with me.</p>
    <p>
      I brought the problem, the requirements, the tests with real pieces, every design decision and the feedback
      rounds. The code was written with Claude Code as an AI pair programmer: Python with a local server in its own Mac
      window (pywebview), Audiveris and homr for reading, music21 and verovio for engraving, PyMuPDF for the PDF and
      MuseScore 4 for the MP3. The interface is plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript.
    </p>
    <ol class="c-timeline">
      <li><time>18 Oct 2026</time><span>Script, second reader, the editor instead of a report.</span></li>
      <li><time>20 Oct</time><span>A test piece with known notes; a Mac app with its own window.</span></li>
      <li><time>22 Oct</time><span>Four harder pieces, from Victoria to Whitacre.</span></li>
      <li><time>23 Oct</time><span>Text removal measured and dropped; three design directions.</span></li>
      <li><time>26 Oct</time><span>The choir folder, brackets, marks on the note head.</span></li>
      <li><time>27 Oct</time><span>Renamed Colla parte; eight fixes from a design review.</span></li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>
